<p><strong>The substitution economics matter here more than in any style we build.</strong> Solid teak runs several times the cost of stained hardwood alternatives, and on painted or upholstered elements the species is invisible anyway. Our standing rule: <strong>solid teak where hands and eyes meet daily</strong> — dining top, bed, the planter's chair — <strong>stained solids for legs and frames, veneer and quality woodgrain laminate for fitted wardrobes and panelling.</strong> Done with matched undertones, the mix is indistinguishable at arm's length and cuts the material bill by 40–60%. Ply grades under the veneer follow the usual law (IS 303 dry, IS 710 wet) — details in our <a href="/blogs/calibrated-vs-normal-plywood/">plywood guide</a>.</p>
<p>Section 06</p>
<h2>Furniture Style: The Pieces That Define the Look</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sofa:</strong> wood-frame with show-wood arms and cane or upholstered panels — the frame must be visible; a fully upholstered modern L hides everything this style is about. Cotton or linen covers in cream, stripe or botanical.</li>
  <li><strong>The planter's chair:</strong> the style's signature — long cane recline, extending arms. One near the best window is worth more atmosphere than any décor budget.</li>
  <li><strong>Dining:</strong> a long solid-top table on turned legs, cane-back chairs (breathable through three-hour Sunday lunches), a bench option for the kids' side.</li>
  <li><strong>TV unit:</strong> the honest modern compromise — a low teak-tone sideboard with framed shutters and brass pulls; the screen sits on it rather than in a panelled wall.</li>
  <li><strong>Coffee table:</strong> a restored trunk, a butler's tray on an X-frame, or a low solid table; this slot loves one genuine antique.</li>
  <li><strong>Beds:</strong> the four-poster where height allows (see the ceiling rule in mistakes), else a tall cane-panel or plain solid headboard. Storage via under-bed drawers, not hydraulic boxes that fatten the profile.</li>
  <li><strong>Wardrobes:</strong> almirah language — framed shutters with beading, brass escutcheons, one mirrored panel — executed as fitted units in veneer/laminate for real-world budgets.</li>
  <li><strong>Storage &amp; display:</strong> the glass-front bookcase is the style's storage hero; a bar cabinet or trolley its social one.</li>
  <li><strong>Heights and shapes:</strong> furniture sits higher than contemporary norms (seat ~450mm, tables to match), legs turned or tapered and always visible; floating and built-in minimal — this style's furniture proudly stands on the floor.</li>
</ul>
<p>Section 07</p>
<h2>Indo-Colonial Room by Room: Build Specs</h2>
<h3>Kitchen</h3>
<p>The period had no fitted kitchens, so we translate rather than copy: <strong>framed shaker-profile shutters with a beading detail</strong> in cream, sage or both (pale uppers, deeper bases), brass cup pulls and knobs, and a <strong>honed black granite counter</strong> — historically right and one of the best-value stones in the market. Backsplash: cream ceramic in a running bond, or the granite carried up 300mm with painted wall above. One open brass rail keeps daily tools period-honest; a plate rack over the sink earns its space. Tall units wear the same framed fronts; corner internals and pantry pull-outs stay invisible and modern inside (our <a href="/blogs/modular-kitchen-storage-ideas/">storage guide</a> applies unchanged). Appliances: black or off-white ranges over stainless where choice exists; the chimney is a slim black or cream model, never a steel pyramid. Layout logic is the same as any kitchen — the <a href="/blogs/kitchen-layouts-guide/">layouts guide</a> covers the triangle math.</p>
<h3>Bedroom</h3>
<p>The bed is the room's monument: four-poster with a light sheer canopy at 10ft ceilings and above; below that, a tall cane-inlay headboard keeps the presence without the posts. The wardrobe wall wears almirah-style framed shutters — the one fitted element that must carry the style's face. Bedside: small turned-leg tables with fabric-shade lamps at 2700K. The dressing corner takes an oval or arch mirror over a small table. Soft furnishing: white cotton base, one block-print layer, one botanical cushion — restraint keeps it bedroom, not boutique-hotel lobby.</p>
<h3>Living room</h3>
<p>Anchor on the seating group, not the TV: wood-frame sofa and two cane chairs in conversation, the planter's chair at the window, a trunk or butler table centre. The feature wall, if any, is a <strong>gallery of botanical prints or vintage maps in slim dark frames</strong> — the style's native art wall — or simple full-height wall panelling with applied moulding painted wall-white. Ceiling: plain, with the <strong>designer ceiling fan as the room's kinetic centrepiece</strong>; a discreet cove may assist but the fan owns the stage. Curtains: white/cream cotton or linen, full height, on dark wood or brass rods with rings — the visible rod is period grammar; bamboo or khus blinds layer beautifully behind them.</p>
<h3>Dining</h3>
<p>The long table under a brass pendant or small chandelier (dimmable), cane-back chairs, and the <strong>crockery almirah</strong> — a glass-front (fluted at premium) display case that turns inherited china into the room's décor. A jute or dhurrie rug sized to hold chairs even when pulled back. One large framed piece or a wall clock completes the zone.</p>
<h3>Bathroom</h3>
<p>Chequerboard or plain warm-stone floor tile, white wainscot-profile wall tile to 1200mm with paint above, a wood-look or painted vanity on legs (BWP core, always), arch or oval mirror, and <strong>PVD antique-brass fittings</strong> — the finish that gives brass's warmth without its tarnish schedule. Lighting: a brass wall bracket either side of the mirror at 3000K beats a downlight overhead in both function and mood.</p>
