<p>Section 08</p>
<h2>Lighting Design: Lamplight, Not Grid Light</h2>
<p>This style predates the recessed downlight, and it shows you why that's a compliment: <strong>pooled warm light from visible fixtures</strong> is half its atmosphere.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Natural light:</strong> the style's first fixture — keep windows tall-dressed but unblocked, sheers filtering the harsh hours, khus or bamboo blinds as the period-correct sun layer. Mirrors opposite windows repeat the bungalow trick of doubling daylight.</li>
  <li><strong>Ambient:</strong> a minimal trimless downlight grid set to 2700K as invisible support only — dimmed to 30% most evenings — plus the fan-light question answered honestly: separate the fan and the light; combined units cheapen both.</li>
  <li><strong>Task:</strong> a brass swing-arm or fabric-shade lamp at every reading seat, under-cabinet strips in the kitchen (3000–4000K, the home's only cool zone), bracket lights at mirrors.</li>
  <li><strong>Accent:</strong> picture lights over the botanical gallery, one uplight in the big plant — small, warm, specific.</li>
  <li><strong>Decorative:</strong> where this style spends — brass pendants, a modest chandelier over dining, standing lamps with pleated shades. Fixtures are furniture here; buy them like it.</li>
  <li><strong>Kelvin rule:</strong> 2700K standard, 3000K ceiling, dimmers on living and dining. The candle-adjacent warmth is what turns teak from brown to glowing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Section 09</p>
<h2>Decorative Elements: A Home That Shows Its Mind</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Plants:</strong> the areca palm is practically a structural element; add a fern on a plant stand and a money plant trailing a bookshelf. Terracotta and brass planters only.</li>
  <li><strong>Wall art:</strong> botanical plates, wildlife lithograph reproductions, vintage maps and monochrome family photographs in slim dark frames — hung gallery-style with disciplined spacing.</li>
  <li><strong>Curtains &amp; blinds:</strong> cotton/linen on visible rods; bamboo or khus behind for the sun.</li>
  <li><strong>Carpets:</strong> jute, dhurries and worn-in Persian-pattern rugs in muted grounds — one per zone, generously sized.</li>
  <li><strong>Artifacts:</strong> blue-and-white ceramics, brass boxes and bells, a chess or backgammon set out and playable, an old clock that ticks. Things with use and history over things with neither.</li>
  <li><strong>Books:</strong> displayed, spines out, actually read — the glass-front bookcase is décor, storage and biography at once.</li>
  <li><strong>Mirrors:</strong> arch and oval forms in dark wood or brass frames, placed to bounce window light.</li>
  <li><strong>Metal accents:</strong> brass throughout — and here's the one liberty the palette allows: brass IS the accent metal, glinting from hardware, lamp stems, planters and frames.</li>
  <li><strong>Textures:</strong> the quartet again — polished grain, cane weave, lime-soft wall, natural fibre — plus leather's patina on one good chair.</li>
</ul>
