    <tr><td><strong>Duplex</strong></td><td>★★★★</td><td>The double-height living zone takes the fan, the gallery wall and the tall proportions superbly</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>2–3BHK apartment</strong></td><td>★★★★</td><td>As the apartment edit — no four-poster under 9.5ft, dark wood rationed, cane and brass carrying the load</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Luxury homes</strong></td><td>★★★★★</td><td>Restored antiques + solid teak + Athangudi zones — heritage as the top-end statement</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Studio / compact</strong></td><td>★★★</td><td>Two hero pieces maximum; the style needs air around its darkness</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Rental homes</strong></td><td>★★★★</td><td>Unusually rental-friendly — the look lives in loose furniture that moves with you</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p><strong>Room sizes:</strong> the honest variable is <strong>height more than area</strong>. At 10ft-plus, everything works. At the standard 9–9.5ft apartment slab, drop the four-poster and heavy pelmets, keep the ceiling plain, and the style adapts gracefully. Open floor plans zone naturally by furniture groups — the seating circle, the dining event, the reading window — without a single partition.</p>
<h3>The buyer profile, honestly drawn</h3>
<p>This style skews 35–60 in our order book — established professionals, doctors and academics, armed-forces and planter-family backgrounds, serious readers, and NRIs building the India of their memory. Income mid-to-high, but with a distinctive pattern: <strong>collectors on instalment</strong> — clients who build the shell with us and add one fine piece a year. <strong>Retired couples</strong> are its happiest owners; the furniture heights are kind and the mood is theirs. <strong>Families with kids:</strong> genuinely good — solid wood survives childhood better than laminate flat-pack, cotton washes, and the home teaches its objects' stories; keep glass-front cases toughened or high. <strong>Pet owners:</strong> the one real conflict is cats versus cane — choose solid-seat pieces or accept the webbing as a scratching casualty. <strong>Who should skip it:</strong> lovers of seamless handle-less surfaces, dark-and-moody palette seekers (this style's darkness lives in furniture, never walls), and anyone unwilling to dust objects — this is a home with things in it, proudly.</p>
